    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Earthquakes commonly rupture along pre-existing fault arrays that have discontinuous surface traces and contain oblique fault intersection. The geometry of these fault segment intersections and the ability of the rupture to jump from one segment to another has a strong control on the cumulative rupture length and the magnitude of an earthquake. Understanding how rupture may propagate though oblique fault intersections may therefore allow one to predict how coseismic rupture may coalesce many fault segments within a given pattern of pre-existing regional faults and produce large earthquakes. Among the fault geometries, arrangements of en- echelon and co-linear fault segments are relatively well documented and studied. Another common geometry of discontinuous faults presents itself in the form of intersecting segments that are oblique in both strike and dip. We use numerical modeling to quantify the tendency for and style of slip that would propagate though different intersection geometries. We then compare these results with observed earthquake ruptures incorporating oblique fault intersections including the 1999 Izmit, Turkey earthquake and the 1976 Songpan, China eathquake sequence.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
